The red case on HEAD, before the fix (2026-10-24, 12:41Z, before `tools/loop/state.sh` was edited; the working
tree carried the new case only):

$ bash tools/loop-selftest.sh --case 'state#recordsTheBaseBranchNotTheCheckout'
  the base is the base branch's commit, not the checkout's: expected '4a8ac4da912f5b52db3f7da78d0b1f5a7f31c38a', got 'c7b02aa61e535b6ef1d2c3dd1b30c5e02a83c361'
FAIL state#recordsTheBaseBranchNotTheCheckout
loop-selftest: 1 of 1 cases failed
(exit 1, 1.3 s)

The two shas are commits of the case's own throwaway repository: the second is the checked-out feature branch's
commit, the first the base branch's — the assertion that failed is the one the defect is about.

Reproducible after the fix by mutation: `tools/loop-mutations.sh --only 56.1` puts the `HEAD` read back in place of
the base branch and the case goes red again (`evidence/revert-check.log`).

The second case, `run#aCheckoutOffTheBaseBranchIsRefusedBeforeThePreScreen`, is red on HEAD for the same root
cause: with no guard the run records the checkout's commit as its base and stops at its first item instead of
refusing the checkout, so its exit code and its recorded reason are not the ones the case asserts. After the fix
the case is green, and mutation `56.2` (the guard disabled) makes it red again — that is the same pre-fix
behaviour, isolated from the recorded-base half.
